---
components: ["LiveSparkline"]
description: Live sparkline draws a stream of samples over a fixed window, batching bursts to one draw per frame and pausing in a hidden tab.
---

<script>
  import { LiveSparkline } from "carbon-components-svelte/viz";
</script>

`LiveSparkline` is part of the data visualization subpackage. Import it from `carbon-components-svelte/viz` and load `carbon-components-svelte/css/viz.css` alongside `all.css`. With a single-theme stylesheet such as `g100.css`, load the matching `viz-g100.css` instead. The subpackage is experimental: its API may change in a minor release.

It is a [Sparkline](/components/Sparkline) that keeps its own values. Give it samples as they arrive and it draws the last `capacity` of them. Every other prop, such as `width`, `height`, `color`, and `fill`, is passed through.

## Basic

Start from a `seed` and set `value` to the latest sample. Each change appends it and the oldest sample falls off the left once the window is full. The chart does not animate: a new sample simply appears, which reads the same under reduced motion.

<LiveSparkline seed={[12, 14, 13, 17, 16, 19, 21, 22, 24]} value={23} capacity={12} label="Signups per minute" />

## Stream

Bind the component and call `push` with a sample, or an array of them, to append samples, including one that equals the last, which `value` would not notice. Pushes within one animation frame are drawn once, so a burst of a hundred samples costs one render. The browser runs no frames in a hidden tab, so a dashboard left in the background holds its samples and draws them when looked at again. Set `paused` to hold them yourself. `update` fires with the values after each draw.

<FileSource src="/framed/LiveSparkline/LiveSparklineStream" />

## Accessibility

With a `label`, the chart is an image named by it; the name does not change with each sample, so nothing is announced repeatedly. Show the latest value as text alongside, as the stream example does with a [BigNumber](/components/BigNumber). Without a label the chart is decorative.
